# Local Netlify folder
.netlify

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ORIGINAL CONTENT — deliberately not published.
#
# This repo is the slide SYSTEM: tokens, elements, templates, scripts and docs.
# The source material is not, because it is EventPipe's own:
#
#   references/        the real decks, including the Series A deck with its
#                      actual revenue, EBITA, retention and capital-plan figures
#   src/assets/        photography of identifiable employees, the company photo,
#                      customer and partner marks, and the recovered slide art
#
# Both were purged from git history, not merely untracked — they had been
# committed since the first commit, so removing them going forward would have
# left them retrievable from any earlier commit.
#
# CONSEQUENCE: a fresh clone has no images, so `img()` will throw on every name
# and Storybook's Templates will not render. That is intended. To restore a
# working checkout, drop `references/` back in and run `pnpm crop:images`
# followed by `pnpm imagery:fetch` — both are reproducible from the manifests
# in scripts/, which is exactly why they are scripts rather than manual copies.
#
# The .ts files under src/assets are code, not content, and stay tracked.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
references/
src/assets/**
!src/assets/**/
!src/assets/**/*.ts

# Review screenshots dropped in by hand while editing slides. Same reason as
# references/: they are pictures of real decks with real figures on them, and
# this repo is public.
edits/

# Deck player build output. Regenerated by `pnpm build:deck`; the deploy nests
# it under storybook-static/deck.
deck-static/

# EXCEPTION: the deck's own supplied images are tracked.
#
# The blanket src/assets ignore exists to keep ORIGINAL DECK CONTENT out of a
# public repo — recovered slide compositions, staff headshots, third-party marks.
# These three are neither: they are images supplied for one presentation, and at
# 248KB total they cost less to commit than to host.
#
# Committing them is what makes them work. Both Netlify and GitHub Pages build
# from a clean checkout, so an ignored image can only be resolved from the image
# host — and that repo is now 319MB, which means every push there costs 12-40
# minutes of Pages rebuild before the picture appears. A tracked image ships with
# the build and cannot 404.
!src/assets/imagery/deck/
!src/assets/imagery/deck/*.jpg
